    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nNissan Altima Refuses to Die, the 2027 Model Year of the Classic Mid-Sizer Kicks Off at $27,790\n6 photos\nPhoto: Nissan\nSurprisingly, the Japanese automaker's local division in America hasn't retired the traditional Nissan Altima, but it now costs a little more - $29,065 with destination and handling included ($1,275).\nThe average transaction price (ATP) for a new vehicle in the United States was $50,562 in September 2026, according to data from Kelley Blue Book, which is up almost one percentage point from August and up 0.7% year-over-year. That’s immediately below the current all-time record of $50,614 set in December 2025.\nClearly, the market continues to absorb inflation and go up, but what that means is that many customers don’t have enough options below that threshold anymore. So, let’s applaud the fact that a carmaker hasn’t killed off another affordable option and that the 2027 Nissan Altima refuses to die and sees another model year, at least.\nEven with the DFC included, the starting price remains below the $30k threshold. On the other hand, the bad news is that, just like it happened with the 2027 Toyota Camry, a direct competitor, things continue to get worse in terms of starting MSRPs because the 2027 Altima is more expensive than its predecessor. Also, the options are shrinking – gone is the all-wheel-drive option on the entry-level SV trim.\nThe 2027 Nissan Altima, which competes in the same (shrinking) segment as the ultra-popular 2027 Toyota Camry along with other foes like the Honda Accord, Hyundai Sonata, and Kia K5, has been rumored to end its career after the 2026 model year. The automaker has now announced the 2027 model year is alive and well, albeit with slightly higher price tags.\nNissan still has the 2027 Sentra starting from $22,990 (sans destination) and the 2026 Altima used to kick off at $27,580 for the base SV model, which had both FWD and AWD. Now it’s $27,790 sans DFC, and only FWD is available. Also gone are the SV Special Edition along with the SR Midnight Edition. Also, the 2026 Altima SR used to start at $28,980, but now it’s $29,190 with FWD and $30,690 with AWD, plus $1,275 for destination and handling.\nLuckily, the price differences aren’t huge, and both the 2027 Altima SV and SR “offer a range of comfort, convenience and safety features that enhance the driving experience, including the reassurance of standard Nissan Safety Shield 360, class-exclusive Intelligent Forward Collision Warning, and available ProPILOT Assist for more convenient highway driving.”\nThe Nissan Altima also has “class-exclusive Zero Gravity seats,” standard Apple CarPlay and Android Auto, optional wireless phone charging, an available 12.3-inch touchscreen infotainment system, and standard Remote Engine Start. The Altima SR goes towards a sporty lifestyle with 19-inch alloy wheels, gloss black exterior trim, carbon fiber center console trim and sports exhaust finishers.\nThere are also standard paddle shifters, Active Ride Control, Intelligent Trace Control and sport suspension (FWD models only). All versions have the same 2.5-liter direct-injection engine rated for 188 horsepower (182 hp with AWD). Front-wheel drive models get fuel economy ratings of up to 26 mpg city and 36 mpg highway.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2027 nissan altima Nissan Altima Nissan Altima mid-size sedan 2027MY pricing US\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT Mercedes-Benz GLE 450 e 4Matic SUV and Coupe Heads to Land Down Under With PHEV For the First Time",
